    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0106",
      "sequence": 118,
      "meaning": "Those opposed to Hari and Hara and without love for dharma never reach Kailāsa even in dreams. Upon that mountain stands an immense banyan, beautiful and ever fresh in every season, with threefold cool breeze and deeply cooling shade—the tree of Śiva’s repose praised by the Vedas. Once the Lord goes beneath it and feels great joy at its sight. Compassionate Śambhu spreads a tiger skin with his own hand and sits naturally. His body is white as jasmine, moon and conch; his arms are long and his garment a sage’s bark-cloth. His youthful reddish feet resemble lotuses, and the radiance of his nails removes devotees’ inner darkness. Serpents and ash adorn Tripurāri; his face surpasses the autumn moon. A crown of matted hair bearing Ganges, vast lotus eyes, blue throat, treasury of beauty and young moon upon his brow make him shine.",
