    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0108",
      "sequence": 120,
      "meaning": "Pārvatī asks Śiva, if he is pleased with her and truly regards her as his servant, to remove her ignorance by narrating Raghunātha’s story in its many forms. One living beneath a wish-tree should not suffer poverty’s pain; therefore moon-adorned Śiva should dispel her heavy intellectual confusion. Sages who teach supreme truth call Rāma beginningless Brahman; Śeṣa, Sarasvatī, Vedas and Purāṇas all sing Raghupati’s virtues, and Śiva reverently repeats ‘Rāma’ day and night. Is this Rāma the very son of Ayodhyā’s king, or some unborn, qualityless and unknowable reality? If he is the prince, how can Brahman become distraught in separation from a woman? His deeds seen and his glory heard leave her understanding greatly perplexed.",
