    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0109",
      "sequence": 121,
      "meaning": "If instead Rāma is some desireless, all-pervading sovereign reality, Pārvatī asks Śiva to explain that too. He should recognize her ignorance without anger and remove her delusion by whatever means. In the forest she saw Rāma’s majesty but, overcome by fear, did not tell Śiva. Even so her impure mind gained no understanding, and she fully suffered that act’s fruit. Some uncertainty still remains, so she pleads with joined hands for grace. Śiva had instructed her in many ways then; remembering that, he should not now be angry. Her former delusion is gone and her mind delights in Rāma’s story. Bowing her head to the earth and joining her lotus hands, she asks serpent-adorned Lord Śiva to narrate Raghubara’s pure glory by distilling the Vedas’ settled conclusion.",
